.TH TERMINAL-SYSTEM 1 "2026-11-03" "terminal-system 1.3.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
terminal-system \- AI-assisted two-panel terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B terminal-system
[\fIclaude\fR|\fIcodex\fR|\fIagy\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Opens a tmux session with two panels. The left panel is a normal shell
where commands run. The right panel accepts instructions in natural
language; the chosen AI engine (Claude Code, Codex or Antigravity)
translates each instruction non-interactively into one or more shell
commands, which are typed into the
left panel in real time.
.PP
The session runs on its own dedicated tmux server, separate from the
user's default one, so the mouse/copy-paste bindings terminal-system
sets up don't affect any other tmux session the user has open.
.SH RIGHT-PANEL COMMANDS
.TP
.B :cmd <command>
Type a shell command manually, without calling the AI engine.
.TP
.B :manual
Prompt for a manual shell command.
.TP
.B :auto readonly|off
Toggle automatic execution of generated commands. Default: off. See
.B SAFETY
below for exactly what auto-mode will and will not run without asking.
"on" is accepted as an alias for "readonly".
.TP
.B :context on|off|show|clear
Toggle whether recent left-panel output is kept and sent to the AI
engine as context for follow-up instructions. Default: off. "show"
prints what is currently stored; "clear" empties it without changing
the on/off setting.
.TP
.B :fix [on|off]
After every command, its exit status is shown; a failure (other than
Ctrl-C, or a plain "no match" from grep/diff/test) is remembered.
.B :fix
sends that command, its exit status and the last 30 lines of left-panel
output (secrets redacted, best effort) to the AI engine, prints the
likely cause and offers a fix command. The fix always asks for
confirmation, even in auto-mode.
.B :fix on
does this automatically after every failure. Default: off.
.TP
.B :follow \fIgoal\fR
Closed loop. The AI proposes one step, it runs in the left panel as
usual (confirmation or read-only auto-mode), the output (secrets
redacted) is sent back and the next step is proposed. Stops when the AI
answers DONE, when a step is cancelled, when the same step is proposed
twice, or after 5 steps.
.TP
.B :explain [\fIcommand\fR]
Without argument, explain the last command that ran and what its output
means (the last 40 lines of the left panel, secrets redacted, are sent
to the AI engine). With an argument, explain that command without
running it.
.TP
.B :watch on|all|off|pause|resume
Open (or close) an observer panel below the instruction panel. It
watches the left panel: after the output has been quiet for 2 seconds
(at most 10 seconds between requests) it sends the latest output
(secrets redacted) to the AI engine and prints advice. It never types
anything. "on" asks the AI only when new output looks like an error
(command not found, permission denied, failed, ...); "all" asks after
every change. Full-screen programs are skipped. Default: off. The first
time, a short notice about what is sent to the AI provider must be
accepted; the answer is remembered in
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/terminal-system/watch-consent .
.B pause
(or the F9 key) stops it from reading anything and discards what appears
meanwhile;
.B resume
(F10) continues.
.TP
.B :engine claude|codex|agy
Switch the AI engine for the rest of the session.
.TP
.B :quit
End the session.
.SH WAITING FOR COMMANDS
While a command runs in the left panel, the instruction panel waits for
it. If the screen shows a prompt (yes/no question, password, passphrase,
"Continue?", pager) focus moves to the left panel and returns when the
command ends. After 10 seconds a "still running" line is printed every
30 seconds; a command that ran 15 seconds or more rings the bell and
shows a tmux message when it finishes. Ctrl-C in the instruction panel
stops waiting without stopping the command. Waiting ends by itself after
30 minutes. Timings can be changed with TS_STATUS_AFTER, TS_STATUS_EVERY,
TS_NOTIFY_AFTER and TS_MAX_WAIT (seconds).
.SH ENVIRONMENT CONTEXT
Every instruction is sent with a short description of the left panel:
distribution name, user, working directory, git branch, shell and, if
the panel is running something other than a shell (for example ssh),
that program. No terminal output is included in it.
.SH SAFETY
Every generated or manual command waits for manual confirmation by
default. Press Enter to execute it, "e" to edit it, "?" to have the AI explain it, or "n" to cancel it. The AI also returns a short explanation and a risk level (low, medium, high) for each command, shown above the prompt; a command rated high, or matching the built-in dangerous patterns, always asks, even in auto-mode, and needs a 4-character code typed instead of Enter.
.PP
In auto-mode, a command runs without confirmation only if it passes
.B both
of the following:
.IP \(bu 2
Its first word is a known read-only program (ls, cat, grep, ps, df, du,
git status/log/diff, systemctl status, journalctl, dpkg -l, apt
list/search/show, and similar), and it contains none of the shell
metacharacters that could turn a read-only command into something else
(redirects, pipes, backticks, \fB$()\fR, \fB;\fR, \fB&\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
It does not match the fixed set of always-dangerous patterns (destructive
rm, mkfs, dd onto a device, fork bombs, any recursive chmod on /,
shutdown/reboot, killall -9, userdel/groupdel on root/sudo/admin,
iptables -F, writes onto common block devices under /dev) -- checked even
though the allowlist above should already exclude them, as a second
layer.
.PP
Anything else -- including any command auto-mode doesn't recognize --
always stops for manual confirmation. This is deliberately conservative:
new instructions and edge cases default to asking rather than to running.
Review generated commands before confirming them regardless of mode.
.PP
When a command is likely to ask for input, such as sudo, ssh, passwd,
an editor, or a pager, focus moves to the left panel so passwords and
other interactive prompts are entered into the shell running the
command. When the command finishes and the left panel returns to the
shell, focus moves back to the instruction panel. Non-interactive
commands keep focus on the instruction panel.
.PP
Left-panel output is never sent to the AI engine, and nothing is kept
between instructions, unless
.B :context on
is used. When on, output is scanned for common secret patterns
(passwords, tokens, API keys, Bearer headers, and similar) before being
stored, but this is best-effort, not a guarantee -- check
.B :context show
before relying on it in a session where sensitive output appeared.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TS_CLAUDE_MODEL
Model name passed to \fBclaude\fR's \fB\-\-model\fR flag. Unset by
default (uses the CLI's own default).
.TP
.B TS_CODEX_MODEL
Model name passed to \fBcodex\fR's \fB\-\-model\fR flag. Unset by
default (uses the CLI's own default).
.TP
.B TS_AGY_MODEL
Model name passed to \fBagy\fR's \fB\-\-model\fR flag. Unset by
default (uses the CLI's own default).
.SH FILES
No configuration files. Session context is kept in process memory for
the current tmux session and is not persisted by terminal-system.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR terminal-router (1)
